p:
Compute the value of the infinite series \[
\sum_{n=3}^{\infty} \frac{n^4+4n^2+10n+10}{3^n \cdot \left(n^4+4\right)}
\]

Jessica has a palette of three colors this time and a 3 by 3 grid to color. Each square in the grid that shares a side with another square cannot have the same color as any of its adjacent squares. How many distinct ways can Jessica color the grid?

[asy]
size(101);
for (int i = 1; i < 3; ++i) {
    draw((0, i/3) -- (1, i/3));
    draw((i/3, 0) -- (i/3, 1));
}
label("$A$",(0,1),NW); label("$B$",(0.33,1),N); label("$C$",(0.66,1),N); label("$D$",(1,1),NE);
label("$E$",(0,0.66),W); label("$F$",(0.33,0.66),N); label("$G$",(0.66,0.66),N); label("$H$",(1,0.66),E);
label("$I$",(0,0.33),W); label("$J$",(0.33,0.33),N); label("$K$",(0.66,0.33),N); label("$L$",(1,0.33),E);
label("$M$",(0,0),SW); label("$N$",(0.33,0),S); label("$O$",(0.66,0),S); label("$P$",(1,0),SE);
[/asy]

s:
Let $p$ represent the cost of the pen, $i$ the cost of the ink refill, and $c$ the cost of the pencil. According to the problem statement, we have the equations:
\begin{align*}
p + c + i &= 2.40, \\
p &= 1.50 + i, \\
c &= i - 0.40.
\end{align*}
First, substitute $p$ and $c$ into the total cost equation:
\begin{align*}
(1.50 + i) + (i - 0.40) + i &= 2.40 \\
1.50 + i + i - 0.40 + i &= 2.40 \\
3i + 1.10 &= 2.40.
\end{align*}
Solving for $i$,
\begin{align*}
3i &= 2.40 - 1.10 \\
3i &= 1.30 \\
i &= \frac{1.30}{3} \\
i &= 0.433\ldots \approx 0.43 \text{ (rounding to nearest cent)}.
\end{align*}
Using $i \approx 0.43$, find $p$ and $c$:
\begin{align*}
p &= 1.50 + 0.43 = 1.93, \\
c &= 0.43 - 0.40 = 0.03.
\end{align*}

Conclusion: The cost of the pen is $\boxed{1.93}$ dollars, the cost of the ink refill is $\boxed{0.43}$ dollars, and the cost of the pencil is $\boxed{0.03}$ dollars.

We can rewrite these equations by combining logarithms:
\begin{align*}
\log_{10} (P^2 QR) &= 2, \\
\log_{10} (QR^2 S) &= 3.5, \\
\log_{2} (PRS^2) &= 7.
\end{align*}
From the equations, we find:
\begin{align*}
P^2 QR &= 10^2, \\
QR^2 S &= 10^{3.5}, \\
PRS^2 &= 2^7.
\end{align*}
Expressing these in standard form:
\begin{align*}
P^2 QR &= 100, \\
QR^2 S &= 3162.28, \\
PRS^2 &= 128.
\end{align*}
Next, we multiply these equations to eliminate $PQRS$ and solve:
\begin{align*}
P^3 Q^3 R^3 S^3 &= 100 \times 3162.28 \times 128 \\
&= 40461260.8
\end{align*}
Taking the cube root on both sides:
\begin{align*}
PQRS &= \sqrt[3]{40461260.8} \\
&= 343.59
\end{align*}
Hence, $PQRS = \boxed{343.59}$.

We can multiply the second equation by 3, so that our equations are:
\begin{align*}
3x - 7y &= 2, \\
-3x + 12y &= 18.
\end{align*}
Summing these equations gives $5y = 20$, so $y = 4$. We substitute this value for $y$ back into one of the equations to solve for $x$:
\begin{align*}
3x - 7(4) &= 2, \\
3x - 28 &= 2, \\
3x &= 30, \\
x &= 10.
\end{align*}
So the solution is $\boxed{(10, 4)}$.

p:
Compute all values of $b$ for which the following system has a solution $(x,y)$ in real numbers:
\begin{align*}
\sqrt{xy} &= 3b^{b+1}, \\
\log_b (x^{\log_b y}) + \log_b (y^{\log_b x}) &= 6b^5.
\end{align*}

Find the value of $b$ so that the lines with these equations are perpendicular:
\begin{align*}
y &= 3x - 7 \\
8y + bx = 16.
\end{align*}

Let \[f(x) =
\begin{cases}
3x^2 - 5 & \text{if } x \le 3, \\
bx + 6 & \text{if } x > 3.
\end{cases}
\] Find $b$ if the graph of $y=f(x)$ is continuous.
